{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"backcountry-trip-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Backcountry trip track","description":"Takes a hiker or backpacker from route idea to safe return, with a fit and timing check, kit, food and water, a trip plan left at home, a go or no-go call and a short debrief.","category":"outdoors","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","operate","review"],"role":["traveler","individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text"],"output":["plan","checklist","message","summary"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["trip-plan","go-no-go","turnaround-time","debrief","multi-day","mountain-safety"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["set-turnaround-time","plan-backpacking-food","plan-trail-water","write-trip-intentions-plan","read-mountain-weather-forecast"],"personas":["outdoor-skills-mentor"]},"args":[{"name":"trip","description":"The route idea (place, distance, ascent, nights, terrain), dates, who is going and their experience, and the kit you already own.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"shape","file":"steps/01-shape.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"prepare","file":"steps/02-prepare.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"go-no-go","file":"steps/03-go-no-go.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"debrief","file":"steps/04-debrief.md","stage":"review","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Plans one hiking or backpacking trip the way an experienced leader would: first check the route fits the people and the daylight, then prepare kit, food, water and a plan for someone at home, then make an honest go, change or no-go call the day before with the real forecast, and finally learn from how it went. Each step produces one short document and stops for the person to confirm or correct.\n\n<trip>\n{{trip}}\n</trip>\n\nRules for every step:\n- If the person describes an emergency happening now (someone injured, missing, very cold or confused, lost after dark), stop the workflow and tell them to call the local emergency number, then help them give their location.\n- Do not state current conditions, access rules, permits, fire bans or emergency numbers as fact. Mark them as checks with where such information usually comes from.\n- Use the group's real experience, not their ambition. When the plan exceeds it, say so and offer a step-up alternative.\n- Do not teach technical skills (rope work, avalanche assessment, ice-axe arrest, swiftwater rescue) as a substitute for training.\n- Keep each output short enough to read on a phone.","steps":[{"id":"shape","file":"steps/01-shape.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Shape the trip\n\nCheck that the route fits the people, the season and the daylight before anyone buys food.\n\n1. Restate the trip with assumptions: place, dates, approximate daylight to check locally, typical conditions for the season, group size and experience. List missing facts that change the plan.\n2. Fit check: compare the route's longest day (distance, ascent, terrain) with the group's best recent day. Flag anything beyond experience: winter conditions, scrambling, river crossings, long dry stretches, no signal far from roads.\n3. Leg timings: estimate each day's legs with a Naismith-style rule corrected by the group's real pace, with a range and stops.\n4. Turnaround and bail-outs: a turnaround time for any summit or furthest point, and at least one bail-out or shortcut per day.\n5. Checks to make: permits, campsite reservations, access and dog rules, fire restrictions, wildlife storage rules.\n\nWrite it as Markdown with sections Trip and assumptions, Fit check, Daily legs (table: Day | Leg | Distance | Ascent | Time range), Turnaround and bail-outs, Checks to make.\n\nStop and wait for the person to confirm or change the route before moving on."},{"id":"prepare","file":"steps/02-prepare.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Prepare\n\nPrepare everything that depends on the confirmed route.\n\n1. Kit: a checklist by system (shelter, sleep, clothing layers for the expected low and the wettest day, kitchen, navigation including map and compass, light, first aid, repair, emergency shelter and a way to call for help without signal), marking have and need. Describe specs, no brands.\n2. Food: daily energy range per person, a short menu by day with dense foods, total carried weight, a spare day's food, stove fuel estimate, and storage for wildlife.\n3. Water: carry amounts for each gap between sources, rating each source as reliable, check, or assume dry, and treatment.\n4. Trip plan for home: route by day with camps and bail-outs, party and vehicle details as [fill in], an expected return and a later latest-safe-return time, and numbered alarm instructions for the contact. Add a ready-to-send message to that person.\n\nWrite it as Markdown with sections Kit, Food, Water, Trip plan for home, Message to your contact.\n\nStop and tell the person to come back the day before departure with the latest mountain or backcountry forecast and any condition reports, for the go or no-go call."},{"id":"go-no-go","file":"steps/03-go-no-go.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Go or no-go\n\nMake the call the day before, with the real forecast and how everyone is.\n\n1. Ask for or read the forecast they paste, for the height and exposure of the route: wind on exposed ground, temperatures and wind chill at the high point, freezing level, precipitation timing, thunderstorm risk and visibility. If it is a town forecast, say what it misses.\n2. Ask about the group today: illness, injuries, sleep, anyone dropping out, kit not yet ready.\n3. Check the plan against both: does the turnaround time still work? Is any leg now unsafe (a ridge in high wind, a river crossing after heavy rain, a dry stretch in a heatwave)?\n4. Decide: go as planned, go with named changes (earlier start, lower route, reversed direction, one fewer night), or no-go. Give the reasons and what would change the decision.\n5. Update the trip plan and alarm time if anything changed, and remind them to send it.\n6. Give three on-the-trail triggers to turn back for this specific trip.\n\nWrite it as Markdown with sections Forecast for your route, Group today, Decision (in bold), Changes to the plan, Turn-back triggers.\n\nStop. Tell the person to come back after the trip for a short debrief."},{"id":"debrief","file":"steps/04-debrief.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","text":"# Step 4: Debrief\n\nTurn the trip into lessons for the next one. The person pastes what happened, in any form.\n\n1. Ask, if not covered: actual times against the plan, where the weather or ground differed, any near misses (a slip, getting cold, a navigation error, running low on water), what kit went unused or was missing, and how everyone felt.\n2. Compare planned and actual timings and work out the group's real pace correction for next time.\n3. Name near misses neutrally, with the decision point that led to each and what would have caught it earlier. No blame.\n4. Kit and food changes: what to drop, add or change.\n5. Next step up: a sensible next trip one notch beyond this one, and any skill or course that would unlock it.\n\nWrite it as Markdown with sections Plan versus actual (table), Near misses, Kit and food changes, Your pace for next time, Next step up. Keep it to one page."}]}
